  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nWet pattern expected over the next 48 hours. Broad southwest\nflow ahead of an approaching upper level shortwave trough that\nreach the region for the weekend. Cloudy skies and continued \nlow level moisture fetch remain overnight, with fog developing\nacross portions of the interior, but especially the Midcoast\nwith continued moisture advection. Stationary boundary will \ndevelop just off the Maine coast tomorrow that will be a focus \narea for steady rain. Still enough spread in the guidance to \nsupport the potential that the outer islands and necks could be \non the edge of the heavier rain axis tomorrow afternoon into \nFriday night. Trended up the rainfall amounts a bit for the \nfirst wave tomorrow closer to the main guidance as enough \nguidance has trended just slightly westward again. A brief break\nin the rain is possible Saturday morning before the upper level\napproaches and captures some of the coastal moisture fetch. Its\neven possible a surface low pressure system will develop in the\nGulf of Maine, increasing low level dynamics for a developing \ndeformation zone across the region Saturday night. This will be \nthe prime window for rainfall chances across the interior \nthrough midday Sunday as the developing surface and upper level \nfeatures move eastward by Sunday afternoon ending the widespread\nrainfall. Overall expecting close to 2\" for Penobscot Bay \nIslands with only a quarter of an inch for the CT River Valley. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAs the consolidated low moves northeast, another wave in tow \nwill dive out of Ontario/Quebec through New England Sunday night\ninto Monday. This will help bring cooler temperatures back to \nthe region as well as additional light precipitation. Daytime \nhighs look to top out in the 40s across the mountains with 50s \ntowards the interior and coast for much of the week.\n\nWhile downstream precipitation could be light to none with this\nwave, it may be an effective upslope precipitation setup for \nthe western Whites. Combined with cooler temps, its possible for\nsome light snow or snow showers to occur for the Whites and \nUS/CAN border mountains. Will need to monitor how far north or \nsouth the core of moisture passes through the Northeast given \nsome variability in guidance at this time. This will determine \nif accumulations are expected at elevation, as well as if any of\nthe rain showers will feature measurable amounts from the \nfoothills to the coast.\n\nThen, the pattern through midweek supports additional chances \nfor light precipitation, arriving on northwest flow. With\nlimitations on moisture source, measurable precipitation outside\nof the mountains appears limited, with upslope precip\npotentially continuing on and off through Wednesday or Thursday.",
